momentum-rate-e77055c1.limits_and_boundary:
In the non-relativistic limit, where the particle's speed is much less than the speed of light, the four-momentum 
p^{$\mu$}
$ approximates to 
mc, m\mathbf{v}
$ and the proper time derivative 
$\frac{d}{d\tau}$ approximates to 
$\frac{1}{\gamma}\frac{d}{dt}$\approx $\frac{d}{dt} (where \gamma is the Lorentz factor and 
$\mathbf{v}
$ is the velocity). The equation then reduces to the classical Lorentz force law 
$\mathbf{F} = q(\mathbf{E} + \mathbf{v} \times \mathbf{B})
$ , where 
$\mathbf{F}
$ is the classical force, 
$\mathbf{E}
$ is the electric field, and 
$\mathbf{B}
$ is the magnetic field. As the particle's velocity approaches the speed of light, relativistic effects become significant, and the momentum 
p^{$\mu$}
$ can no longer be approximated by its non-relativistic form.

p.description:
The linear momentum of the oscillating mass, given by the product of its mass and velocity (
 p = mv 
). It represents the 'quantity of motion' of the object.
